/A 2230 0 R
/ADBE_FT 2231 0 R
/C /adbe_style_002002
/ID (AD260921-1009-1222-ADBE-000000006349)
/K 0
/P 42 0 R
/Pg 16 0 R
/S /Figure
/Alt (Figure 3: Building Trait Causal Models. \(A.\) Many existing approaches in functional genomics focus on integration between pairs of data pillars to connect biological layers. \(B.\) A first generation TCM for red blood cell traits explicitly considered directions of effects within perturbation graphs and for genetic associations; \(C.\) Example TCM framework incorporating quantitative measurements across 3 data pillars. \(D.\) Future extensions of the TCM framework could incorporate further aspects of trait biology including: \(1.\) jointly modeling genetic effects in multiple cell types; \(2.\) additional layers of regulation between biological entities; \(3.\) feedback both upstream and downstream of traits. The image shows four panels labeled A, B, C, and D, each illustrating different aspects and future directions of Trait Causal Models \(TCMs\) in genomics.)
/Contents (Figure 3: Building Trait Causal Models. \(A.\) Many existing approaches in functional genomics focus on integration between pairs of data pillars to connect biological layers. \(B.\) A first generation TCM for red blood cell traits explicitly considered directions of effects within perturbation graphs and for genetic associations; \(C.\) Example TCM framework incorporating quantitative measurements across 3 data pillars. \(D.\) Future extensions of the TCM framework could incorporate further aspects of trait biology including: \(1.\) jointly modeling genetic effects in multiple cell types; \(2.\) additional layers of regulation between biological entities; \(3.\) feedback both upstream and downstream of traits. The image shows four panels labeled A, B, C, and D, each illustrating different aspects and future directions of Trait Causal Models \(TCMs\) in genomics.)
>>
endobj
